            <ArticleTitle>Diphyllobothrium latum infection in a child with recurrent abdominal pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>451-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3345/kjp.2015.58.11.451</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diphyllobothrium latum infection in humans is not common in Republic of Korea. We report a case of fish tapeworm infection in a 10-year-old boy after ingestion of raw perch about 8 months ago. The patient complained of recurrent abdominal pain and watery diarrhea. A tapeworm, 85 cm in length, without scolex and neck, was spontaneously discharged in the feces of the patient. The patient was treated with 15-mg/kg single dose praziquantel, and follow-up stool examination was negative after one month. There was no evidence of relapse during the next six months.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
